s not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alabama's Senate race is an open seat now that Tommy Tuberville has left it to run for governor, and it remains firmly Republican on paper. The state leans about fifteen points to the right, and every major race rating published in late September places the contest in the Safe or Solid Republican column. The only public poll, conducted in July, showed Barry Moore leading Everett Wess by fifteen points among likely voters.\n\nMoore, a congressman backed by Donald Trump in the primary, holds an overwhelming financial edge: roughly $3.8 million raised plus about $16.5 million in outside support, against a Democratic operation that has raised about $80,000. Prediction markets put Democratic chances between one and four percent. Wess continues to campaign against long odds, and an independent candidate has even urged him to withdraw. A controversy over Moore's support for a $5,000 election-related offer has drawn headlines but has not changed the race's fundamental shape.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0155, "margin": -14.03}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.004, "margin": -19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.018, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0262, "margin": -15.0}]}